I1_Q1.text:
{
  "response": "Legal certainty in public-sector AI means that people can reasonably understand the legal rules governing an AI-assisted decision, anticipate how those rules may affect them, and identify how to challenge an unlawful outcome. Public authorities must remain governed by accessible, sufficiently precise law rather than by undisclosed or unpredictably changing computational rules. Certainty does not mean guaranteeing a particular outcome or eliminating administrative discretion; it means making the exercise of public power sufficiently foreseeable and legally controllable.\n\nThis definition draws on the European Court of Human Rights’ judgment in The Sunday Times v. United Kingdom (No. 1), application no. 6538/74 (1979), paragraph 49: law must be accessible and sufficiently precise to allow people, with appropriate advice if necessary, to foresee the consequences of their conduct. The Venice Commission’s Rule of Law Checklist, CDL-AD(2016)007, likewise identifies accessibility, foreseeability, stability, consistency, and respect for legitimate expectations as aspects of legal certainty. Applying these principles to AI requires attention both to the governing law and to how the system operationalizes it.\n\nIn practice, legal certainty requires:\n\n1. An identifiable legal basis and bounded authority. The authority should be able to identify the rules authorizing the decision, the system’s permitted purposes, and the limits on data use and discretion. A procurement contract or a vendor’s model specification cannot substitute for legal authority.\n\n2. Accessible rules and meaningful notice. People should be able to discover when AI materially influences a decision, which legal criteria govern it, and what consequences may follow. Relevant criteria must not be silently replaced by statistical proxies. This does not necessarily require publishing source code; it requires information sufficient to understand and contest the exercise of public power.\n\n3. Traceable, reasoned, and accountable decisions. Authorities need records sufficient to reconstruct the system’s contribution, explain the legal and factual basis of an individual outcome, and identify who is responsible. Human involvement must provide genuine scrutiny where required, not merely endorse a machine-generated recommendation.\n\n4. Predictable administration and effective challenge. Material system changes should be controlled and documented, comparable cases should be handled consistently unless a difference is legally justified, and affected people should have practical routes to correct errors and obtain independent review.\n\nThese requirements have concrete, but jurisdiction-specific, legal expressions. The GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679, provides relevant transparency and access duties in Articles 13–15 and protections concerning certain solely automated decisions with legal or similarly significant effects in Article 22. Article 22 is not a blanket prohibition on all public-sector AI: its scope, exceptions, and safeguards matter. The EU AI Act, Regulation (EU) 2024/1689, adds requirements for covered high-risk systems concerning risk management, logging, human oversight, and deployment; Article 27 requires fundamental-rights impact assessments for specified deployers, and Article 86 provides a qualified right to explanation for certain individual decisions. Its obligations have phased application dates and do not all apply to every public-sector system.\n\nLegal certainty is absent when a person cannot determine why benefits were denied, why they were selected for investigation, which rules actually controlled the outcome, or who can correct an error. Warning signs include secret eligibility criteria, undocumented model changes, vendor secrecy that obstructs review, and officials unable to justify decisions independently of a score. The District Court of The Hague’s SyRI judgment, ECLI:NL:RBDHA:2020:1878 (2020), illustrates these concerns: it held the challenged welfare-fraud detection framework incompatible with Article 8 ECHR, emphasizing deficiencies in transparency and safeguards in its proportionality analysis. It did not prohibit every algorithmic fraud-detection system.\n\nUltimately, legal certainty concerns the knowability and contestability of legally constrained public power. Accuracy, fairness, and technical explainability can support it, but none alone establishes it.",
