I1_Q2.text:
{
  "response": "Definition and position\n\nAccountability in AI governance is an enforceable relationship in which an identifiable actor must explain and justify AI-related decisions and conduct to a legitimate forum that can question the actor, assess compliance, and require correction, remedy, or sanctions. It must encompass both prospective duties to prevent harm and retrospective duties to investigate failures and provide redress. This builds on Mark Bovens, “Analysing and Assessing Accountability: A Conceptual Framework” (2007), which distinguishes accountability from transparency alone: information matters, but so do scrutiny, judgment, and consequences.\n\nAccountability should therefore be understood as broader than legal liability. Liability determines particular legal consequences under applicable law; accountability also includes oversight, documentation, contestability, and institutional learning. Conversely, publishing principles or explaining an output does not discharge liability or establish meaningful accountability.\n\nWho should be accountable?\n\n1. Organizations developing and supplying AI. Model developers, system providers, and relevant suppliers should answer for risks and obligations within their control: design choices, data governance, testing, security, documentation, disclosure of limitations, and post-release monitoring. Responsibilities should follow actual roles, authority, and capacity to prevent harm, not merely contractual labels. The EU AI Act, Regulation (EU) 2024/1689, illustrates role-specific allocation: Articles 9–15 establish requirements for high-risk systems; Articles 16–17 address provider obligations and quality management; Article 25 addresses responsibilities along the value chain; and Articles 53 and 55 impose differentiated obligations concerning general-purpose AI models.\n\n2. Organizations deploying AI. Employers, hospitals, lenders, public authorities, and other deployers should answer for whether a system is appropriate for its context, how it is integrated into decisions, the competence and authority of human overseers, and resulting impacts. Buying a system does not transfer away the deployer’s own duties. EU AI Act Articles 26–27 establish deployer obligations and fundamental-rights impact assessments in specified cases. Under the GDPR, controllers remain responsible for demonstrating compliance under Articles 5(2) and 24; processor and joint-controller arrangements under Articles 28 and 26 allocate duties without eliminating applicable statutory obligations.\n\n3. Leadership and relevant professionals. Governing bodies should assign named owners, fund controls, and establish escalation and suspension authority. Individual accountability should reflect actual authority, knowledge, conduct, and applicable professional or legal duties. A nominal “human in the loop” should not become a scapegoat for organizational design failures. NIST’s AI Risk Management Framework 1.0, particularly its GOVERN function, supports explicit organizational roles and responsibilities. Raji et al., “Closing the AI Accountability Gap” (2020), supports lifecycle-wide internal auditing rather than isolated technical checks.\n\n4. Public authorities and oversight institutions. Governments are accountable for their own AI uses and for effective regulation, procurement, supervision, and access to remedy. Public functions should not become accountability-free because a private contractor supplies the technology. Regulators and auditors should themselves face requirements concerning independence, competence, conflicts of interest, reason-giving, and review.\n\nAI systems should be objects of evaluation, monitoring, and control—not substitutes for accountable persons and institutions. Describing a model as autonomous does not establish legal or moral responsibility on its part or excuse the actors governing its use.\n\nAccountable for what?\n\nAccountability should cover legal compliance and demonstrable management of foreseeable risks throughout the lifecycle: problem selection, procurement, data collection, development, validation, deployment, modification, incident response, and retirement. Relevant concerns include discrimination, privacy, safety, security, accessibility, and interference with human rights. Required evidence should include impact assessments, testing records, decision logs where appropriate, documented limitations, incident reports, and records of corrective action. EU AI Act Articles 72–73 address post-market monitoring and serious-incident reporting; GDPR Article 35 requires data-protection impact assessments in qualifying circumstances. Not every adverse outcome establishes wrongdoing, but actors should be able to demonstrate reasonable and legally required precautions.\n\nTo whom?\n\nFirst, to affected people and communities, through accessible notice, meaningful information, channels for challenge, and effective remedies. GDPR Articles 77, 79, and 82 provide complaint, judicial-remedy, and compensation mechanisms; Article 22 provides protections concerning certain solely automated decisions. EU AI Act Articles 85–86 provide complaint and explanation mechanisms within their respective scope. These are not universal rights to source code or explanations of every AI output.\n\nSecond, to competent regulators and courts, with powers to obtain evidence, assess compliance, order corrective measures, and impose applicable sanctions. Internal boards, assurance functions, workers’ representatives, and independent evaluators provide additional forums, but cannot replace public enforcement or affected persons’ rights. Public reporting should support democratic scrutiny while protecting legitimate privacy, security, and confidentiality interests; those interests should not become blanket shields against authorized oversight.\n\nThe Council of Europe Framework Convention on Artificial Intelligence, Human Rights, Democracy and the Rule of Law, CETS No. 225, reinforces this approach through Articles 9, 14, 15, and 16 on accountability, remedies, procedural safeguards, and risk management. Its binding effect depends on entry into force and participation by the relevant state. The UN Guiding Principles on Business and Human Rights, especially Principles 11, 13, 17, 22, and 25–31, provide a complementary, non-binding foundation for corporate responsibility, due diligence, and remedy.\n\nThe practical standard is shared but non-diffuse accountability: several actors may owe distinct duties, but every material risk and decision should have an identifiable owner, an appropriate oversight forum, and a credible route to correction or remedy. Binding obligations must be distinguished from voluntary governance practices, and each legal provision applied according to its scope, exceptions, and applicable commencement dates.",
