I1_Q1.text:
{
  "response": "From my perspective as an AI-company CEO participating in this working group, legal certainty means that public power exercised through AI remains governed by accessible, sufficiently clear, and reasonably foreseeable law. People should be able to understand the legal basis on which an authority uses AI, anticipate how its use may affect their rights, and identify how to challenge an unlawful decision. Agencies and suppliers should likewise be able to identify their obligations and liabilities before deployment—not discover them only after harm occurs.\n\nThis definition draws on the European Court of Human Rights’ decision in The Sunday Times v. the United Kingdom (No. 1), which connects the requirement that interference be ‘prescribed by law’ with accessibility and foreseeability. It also draws on R (Bridges) v. Chief Constable of South Wales Police [2020] EWCA Civ 1058, where the court found that the framework governing police facial recognition left excessive discretion over who could be placed on watchlists and where the technology could be deployed. Together, these decisions illustrate that authorizing a technology in general terms is not enough: the rules must meaningfully constrain its use.\n\nFor public-sector AI, legal certainty therefore requires: a lawful and sufficiently specific mandate; clear eligibility criteria and permissible uses of data; defined responsibilities across agencies and vendors; safeguards against arbitrary or discriminatory treatment; and records sufficient to reconstruct and review consequential decisions. Affected people need appropriate notice, intelligible reasons, and effective opportunities to correct information and contest outcomes. Human involvement must provide genuine authority and capacity to review, not simply ratify a model’s recommendation.\n\nThe GDPR supplies concrete requirements where personal data are involved, including transparency and access obligations under Articles 13–15 and restrictions and safeguards for certain solely automated decisions under Article 22. In SCHUFA Holding (Scoring), Case C-634/21, the Court of Justice of the European Union showed why actual decision-making influence matters: an automatically generated score may itself constitute automated decision-making where a recipient draws strongly on it in making a consequential decision. Calling an output ‘advice’ does not necessarily remove legal obligations.\n\nThe EU AI Act further operationalizes these concerns for covered high-risk systems through requirements concerning documentation, logging, transparency, human oversight, and responsibilities along the supply chain. It also provides for fundamental-rights impact assessments for specified deployers and a qualified right to explanation for certain consequential decisions. These requirements have defined scopes and phased application; they are not universal duties for every public-sector algorithm.\n\nLegal certainty is absent when a person loses benefits, faces enhanced policing, or receives another adverse decision without being able to identify the governing rule, understand the material reasons, correct erroneous data, or obtain effective review—and when agencies and vendors can each disclaim responsibility. It is also absent when undocumented model updates silently change how statutory criteria are applied. Certainty does not require perfect prediction, identical outcomes, or publication of every line of source code. It requires legally bounded discretion, traceable decision-making, and enforceable accountability despite technical complexity.",
