I1_Q2.text:
{
  "response": "As an AI-company CEO, I would define accountability as an enforceable relationship in which identifiable people and organizations must explain and justify their AI-related decisions to an appropriate forum, submit to scrutiny, and face corrective action or consequences when their obligations are breached. This follows Mark Bovens’s distinction between accountability as a concrete actor–forum relationship and accountability as a general aspiration. Transparency and ethical principles support accountability, but do not substitute for it.\n\nWHO SHOULD BE ACCOUNTABLE?\n\nAccountability should follow legal duties, decision-making authority, control, and contribution to risk across the AI lifecycle. It should be shared without becoming diffuse.\n\n1. Developers and model providers should answer for risks within their control: design choices, training-data governance, evaluations, security, documentation, known limitations, and appropriate support for downstream users. Under the EU AI Act, high-risk-system providers have obligations concerning risk management, documentation, logging, human oversight, quality management, and corrective action (Articles 9–17 and 20). General-purpose-model providers have distinct obligations under Article 53, with additional duties for models with systemic risk under Article 55. These categories should not be conflated.\n\n2. Deployers should answer for whether a system is appropriate for its actual use, how it is integrated, whether operators have meaningful authority and competence, and how affected people can challenge outcomes. The EU AI Act assigns deployer duties in Article 26 and requires fundamental-rights impact assessments for specified deployers under Article 27. Procurement should not transfer away responsibility for deployment decisions.\n\n3. Organizations, boards, and executives should answer for governance, resources, incentives, and escalation decisions. In my company, I would require named executive owners, independent challenge, documented release decisions, and authority to suspend unsafe systems. These are governance commitments, not a claim that existing law automatically makes every CEO personally liable. Personal legal liability should depend on the applicable law and individual conduct.\n\n4. Governments and regulators should answer for their own AI uses and for the fairness, competence, and effectiveness of oversight. A public authority cannot outsource its public-law responsibilities merely by buying a private system.\n\nAI systems themselves should not be the primary bearers of accountability. Assigning responsibility to a model must not become a way to shield the people and institutions that build, sell, deploy, and govern it. The EU AI Act’s allocation of obligations across the value chain, including Article 25, supports this institutional approach.\n\nACCOUNTABLE FOR WHAT?\n\nAccountability should cover both conduct and consequences: lawful purposes, foreseeable risks, reasonable preventive measures, truthful claims about capabilities, ongoing monitoring, incident response, and remediation. It is neither a guarantee of zero harm nor merely proof that a checklist was completed. Compliance evidence must be tested against actual performance and impacts.\n\nFor personal-data processing, GDPR Articles 5(2) and 24 expressly require controllers to demonstrate compliance. Articles 26 and 28 distinguish joint-controller and processor responsibilities; Article 35 requires impact assessment for processing likely to create high risks. These distinctions show why responsibilities must be allocated according to actual roles, rather than treating every participant identically.\n\nOperationally, I would require traceable decisions, meaningful evaluations, documented limitations, incident reporting, and independent assurance proportionate to risk. NIST’s AI Risk Management Framework organizes this work through Govern, Map, Measure, and Manage. Raji and colleagues’ work on end-to-end internal algorithmic auditing explains how organizational processes can make accountability practical. Neither voluntary framework compliance nor an audit should provide blanket immunity.\n\nACCOUNTABLE TO WHOM?\n\nFirst, to affected people, through accessible notice, appropriate explanations, opportunities to contest consequential decisions, and effective remedies. GDPR Article 22 provides safeguards for specified solely automated decisions, while Article 82 provides compensation for qualifying damage. EU AI Act Articles 85 and 86 provide complaint and explanation mechanisms within their respective scopes; they are not universal rights covering every AI output.\n\nSecond, to empowered regulators and courts, with access to sufficient evidence and authority to order correction or impose sanctions. Third, to boards, workers, customers, and the public through appropriately designed oversight and reporting. Shareholder accountability alone is insufficient when risks fall on others.\n\nThe OECD AI Principles’ accountability principle supports role-sensitive responsibility and traceability. The UN Guiding Principles on Business and Human Rights reinforce corporate responsibility to respect rights, conduct due diligence, and provide or cooperate in remediation, alongside states’ duties. Both are nonbinding frameworks, unlike applicable legislation. My bottom line is that every consequential AI decision needs an identifiable responsible institution, a competent forum for challenge, and a credible route to correction and remedy.",
