I1_Q3.text:
{
  "response": "From my perspective as an AI-company CEO participating in this working group, enforceability means that a rule can be translated into a credible legal determination and, where necessary, a corrective consequence. A company should be able to understand its obligations, an authorized institution should be able to investigate compliance, and a court or regulator should be able to require a remedy. The existence of a published principle—or even a statutory prohibition—is not sufficient to establish practical enforceability.\n\nI would assess a proposed rule against six requirements:\n\n1. An identifiable duty-holder and trigger. The rule must specify who is responsible—such as the model provider, system deployer, or importer—and what activities or risks trigger the obligation. Responsibility should track control and access to relevant information, rather than defaulting entirely to either developers or downstream users.\n\n2. A legally assessable obligation. 'AI must be trustworthy' is generally aspirational standing alone. A requirement to document specified risk assessments, test for defined hazards, retain relevant records, and address identified deficiencies is more readily enforceable. This does not mean every obligation needs a numerical threshold: contextual standards such as reasonable care can be enforceable when legislation, guidance, and precedent make their application sufficiently predictable.\n\n3. Obtainable evidence. Authorities need lawful access to the information necessary to establish compliance or breach. Technical documentation, incident reports, appropriate logs, and independent testing can make obligations verifiable. Access must include safeguards for privacy, security, and confidential information; public disclosure of model weights is not a prerequisite for enforceability.\n\n4. An empowered and capable institution. A regulator needs jurisdiction, investigatory powers, technical expertise, resources, and cross-border cooperation. A nominally powerful regulator without access to evidence or qualified staff produces a gap between law on the books and law in action.\n\n5. Effective consequences and remedies. These may include corrective orders, restrictions on deployment, withdrawal from the market, injunctions, or proportionate penalties. Compensation may also matter where a legal cause of action exists. Fines alone are insufficient if a company can treat them as a predictable operating expense.\n\n6. Procedural legitimacy. Decisions need reasoned explanations, opportunities to contest evidence, and independent review. An enforceable regime must constrain both regulated companies and enforcement authorities.\n\nThe EU AI Act illustrates this architecture: its high-risk-system requirements include risk management and documentation, while conformity assessment, post-market monitoring, incident reporting, market surveillance, and penalties create mechanisms for checking and compelling compliance. The GDPR provides another useful model: Article 5(2) requires controllers to demonstrate compliance with data-protection principles; Articles 58 and 83 supply supervisory powers and administrative fines. Neither instrument proves that enforcement will always be effective, but both go beyond declarations of values.\n\nIn the United States, Section 5 of the Federal Trade Commission Act shows that AI-specific legislation is not always necessary: deceptive AI claims or unfair practices may fall under existing law, subject to its legal elements and jurisdictional limits. Conversely, the NIST AI Risk Management Framework is expressly voluntary. It supports implementation and evaluation but does not independently create generally binding legal duties; particular requirements can become binding through incorporation into contracts or applicable legal instruments.\n\nTwo theoretical frameworks inform my position. Roscoe Pound's distinction between 'law in books' and 'law in action' directs attention to actual institutional performance. Ayres and Braithwaite's responsive regulation supports graduated intervention, backed by credible escalation rather than reliance on voluntary cooperation alone.\n\nMy practical test is: who must do what, what evidence establishes breach, who can obtain that evidence and decide, what remedy follows, and how can the decision be challenged? If those questions have no workable answers, the proposal is closer to an aspiration than an enforceable governance rule.",
