I1_Q1.text:
{
  "response": "Legal certainty (Rechtssicherheit), as a cornerstone of the rule of law and administrative justice, dictates that the law and administrative actions must be clear, precise, predictable, and accessible, ensuring that individuals can foresee the legal consequences of their actions and contest public authority. When applied to artificial intelligence (AI) systems deployed in public decision-making—such as welfare distribution, fraud detection, taxation, and criminal justice—legal certainty requires that algorithmic determinations adhere strictly to statutory mandates rather than creating de facto policy through opaque statistical inferences. Substantively, it requires four core elements: (1) Statutory Competence and Foreseeability: An explicit legislative basis demarcating the scope, criteria, and boundaries of the algorithmic tool, ensuring that executive agencies do not exercise arbitrary discretion; (2) Explainability and Reason-Giving: Sufficient technical and procedural intelligibility to allow affected individuals to know the specific factual and legal grounds upon which a decision rests, thereby satisfying constitutional due process and the duty to give reasons; (3) Non-Arbitrariness and Auditability: Robust verification that the system yields consistent, reproducible, and verifiable results across comparable cases without arbitrary variance or latent bias; and (4) Contestability and Effective Judicial Protection: Mechanisms enabling both the individual and reviewing courts to meaningfully scrutinize the logic, input data, and normative trade-offs embedded within the system to ensure compliance with the law.\n\nConversely, the absence of legal certainty in algorithmic public administration produces structural arbitrariness, erosion of procedural rights, and systemic injustice. Its absence manifests when individuals are subjected to adverse governmental actions generated by proprietary or statistically uninterpretable 'black-box' models, leaving them unable to determine why they were selected, whether the law was correctly applied, or how to mount an effective legal defense. A quintessential manifestation occurred in the Netherlands with the System Risk Indication (SyRI), where the District Court of The Hague invalidated a governmental algorithmic fraud-detection system because its opacity, deep data-scraping, and lack of verifiable criteria violated the right to private life under Article 8 of the European Convention on Human Rights (ECHR), precisely because individuals could not reasonably anticipate how their data was evaluated. Similarly, in the United States, State v. Loomis highlighted the constitutional tension surrounding legal certainty when proprietary recidivism scoring algorithms (COMPAS) are used in criminal sentencing without permitting the defendant full access to the proprietary software's underlying weights and logic, thereby testing the bounds of procedural due process. Without legal certainty, public administration devolves into algorithmic fatalism, in which statutory entitlements are quietly supplanted by probabilistic correlations, eviscerating the constitutional guarantee of accessible, predictable, and accountable government.",
