I1_Q2.text:
{
  "response": "In AI governance, accountability is defined as a relational and institutional obligation requiring human agents and legal entities to monitor, explain, justify, and bear legal and moral consequences for the design, deployment, and outcomes of artificial intelligence systems. Drawing from Mark Bovens' foundational public accountability paradigm, accountability is not merely passive answerability or post-hoc blame; it constitutes a structured forum-actor relationship comprising three stages: information provision (disclosing how a system functions and decisions were made), debate (justifying choices against legal and ethical benchmarks), and sanctions or redress (imposing remedies, fines, or operational halts when standards are breached).\n\n1. Who is accountable:\nAccountability must strictly attach to natural persons and legal entities across the AI lifecycle—including system developers, data curators, deployers/operators, and executive leadership—rather than the AI software itself. Granting autonomous systems independent legal personhood obscures liability and creates what Helen Nissenbaum termed the 'problem of many hands,' wherein collective complexity dilutes individual responsibility. As formulated in the EU AI Act and OECD guidelines, accountability is distributed along the value chain based on the degree of control, foreseeability, and operational authority exercised by each entity.\n\n2. For what:\nActors are accountable for: (a) Procedural compliance, including data quality, bias audits, continuous risk management, cybersecurity, and regulatory adherence; (b) Substantive outcomes, including discriminatory impacts, fundamental rights infringements, deceptive outputs, physical harm, and economic damages caused by system deployment; and (c) Explainability and contestability, ensuring decisions can be scrutinized, audited, and challenged by affected parties.\n\n3. To whom:\nAccountable actors owe duties to: (a) Affected individuals, who possess rights to explanation, non-discrimination, and legal remedy (e.g., under GDPR Articles 15 and 22); (b) Sectoral regulators and market surveillance authorities, empowered to audit compliance, demand documentation, and impose administrative penalties; and (c) Democratic institutions and the public, upholding societal trust, public interest, and collective safety.",
