I1_Q1.text:
{
  "response": "Legal certainty for AI in public decision-making is the rule-of-law condition in which the legal basis, decision rules, role of the AI system, and limits of official discretion are accessible and foreseeable before and after a decision, so individuals can plan, understand, and contest how public authority is exercised. It draws on Joseph Raz's account of the rule of law as requiring prospective, open, clear, and stable rules, and Lon Fuller's principles of legality requiring publicity, clarity, and consistency. It also reflects the ECHR requirement in Sunday Times v. United Kingdom that law be accessible and foreseeable. It does not mean disclosing proprietary source code; it means enough transparency, reasons, record-keeping, human accountability, and review to make the exercise of public power predictable and non-arbitrary. It requires: (1) a clear legal basis and statutory criteria for using AI, without delegating public decision-making to an opaque private model; (2) accessible, specific rules about when and how AI is used, including data, model version, and discretion; (3) consistency and stability, with version control and change management; (4) transparency and reasons sufficient to explain the decision and the AI's role, as reflected in EU AI Act Articles 13, 14, and 86 for high-risk systems and GDPR Articles 13-15 and 22; (5) human oversight and accountable officials, plus logging and audit under EU AI Act Articles 12, 14, and 26; (6) contestability, effective remedy, and fair procedure, including ECHR Articles 6 and 13; (7) data protection, accuracy, and non-discrimination; and (8) safeguards against automation bias and unlawful retroactive model changes. The Council of Europe Framework Convention on AI reinforces legality, transparency, accountability, and human rights in AI. Absence looks like hidden or proprietary scoring used in benefits, policing, migration, or sentencing without notice; no meaningful explanation of the factors or the AI's weight; secret or shifting model updates; no reliable record; officials treating scores as conclusive; no human review; inability to correct data or appeal; inconsistent outcomes for like cases; or use beyond legal authority. State v. Loomis illustrates the due-process danger when a proprietary risk score is used in sentencing, even though the Wisconsin court found no violation because the score was not determinative and other safeguards existed. Technological Due Process scholarship by Citron and rule-of-law analyses of automated administration by Coglianese and Lehr warn that automation can erode notice, reasons, and individualized justice. In short, legal certainty is present when AI-assisted public decisions are legally authorized, foreseeable, reasoned, documented, humanly accountable, and contestable; it is absent when opacity and automation make public power unpredictable and unreviewable.",
