I1_Q3.text:
{
  "response": "Enforceability in AI regulation means that an AI-related norm is binding and backed by institutions that can identify violations, require compliance, impose sanctions or remedies, and provide redress. It has formal and practical dimensions. Formally, the norm must be valid law — a treaty, statute, regulation, or a standard incorporated by law — and must specify determinate obligations, addressees, triggers, and consequences. Practically, there must be monitoring, inspection, audit, complaint and investigation powers, sanctions, courts or tribunals, and sufficient resources and expertise. Enforceability is not binary; legalization theory captures it along dimensions of obligation, precision, and delegation. A rule is merely aspirational when it states a goal or principle without a duty-bearer, measurable conduct, verification mechanism, sanction, or avenue for redress — for example, voluntary ethics codes and many declarations or recommendations. Soft law can become enforceable by incorporation into contracts, procurement, technical standards, or legislation; the EU AI Act's codes of practice are a hybrid example because adherence can evidence compliance while the Act's binding duties and penalties remain the hard core.\n\nKey distinctions include: (1) binding legal form versus hortatory language; (2) determinate and measurable obligations versus vague aspirations such as 'AI should be trustworthy'; (3) identifiable duty-holders versus diffuse responsibility; (4) monitoring, audit, reporting, and conformity assessment versus no verification; (5) sanctions and remedies such as fines, injunctions, damages, market withdrawal, or criminal liability versus no consequence; (6) an adjudicative forum or regulator with powers versus no forum; and (7) due process and appeal rights, because enforceability must itself be lawful.\n\nExamples: The EU AI Act (Regulation (EU) 2024/1689) is enforceable through prohibitions in Article 5, high-risk obligations in Articles 8-27, transparency duties in Article 50, general-purpose AI rules in Articles 51-56, market surveillance by national authorities and the AI Office, and Article 99 penalties reaching €35 million or 7% of worldwide turnover. The GDPR supplies the enforcement model: Article 83 administrative fines, Article 77 complaints, Article 79 judicial remedies, and Article 82 compensation. In the US, FTC Act §5 enforcement against deceptive or unfair AI claims, Title VII disparate-impact liability, the Colorado AI Act (SB 24-205) with attorney-general enforcement, and NYC Local Law 144's bias-audit requirement illustrate varied enforcement hooks. Loomis v. Wisconsin (Wis. 2016) is not an AI statute but shows procedural due-process limits: COMPAS risk scores could be used only with warnings and not as the sole basis for sentencing, illustrating how adjudicative safeguards make algorithmic evidence contestable. Internationally, the Council of Europe Framework Convention on AI (2024) is binding but relies on domestic implementation and follow-up, making its enforceability weaker than EU regulation. The OECD AI Principles and UNESCO Recommendation on AI Ethics are policy frameworks without binding enforcement.\n\nTheoretical frameworks: Hart's primary and secondary rules explain that enforceability depends on rules of recognition, change, and adjudication. Fuller's inner morality of law requires clarity, publicity, non-retroactivity, and congruence between rules and official action; aspirational rules often fail these. Legalization theory (Abbott et al.) treats obligation, precision, and delegation as the dimensions separating hard from soft law. Responsive regulation (Ayres and Braithwaite) adds an enforcement pyramid with credible escalating sanctions and cooperative compliance. Thus enforceable AI regulation is not merely a stated value; it is a legally valid, determinate, institutionally monitored, sanction-backed, and reviewable norm.",
