I1_Q2.text:
{
  "response": "As the CEO of a major AI company, I would define accountability in AI governance as a binding and verifiable allocation of answerability across the AI lifecycle: named human and corporate actors must be able to explain, justify, and remedy the decisions and harms associated with AI systems they develop, procure, deploy, or operate, and must face consequences through regulators, courts, customers, and affected communities. Accountability is not liability alone and is not a property of a model; it is a governance relationship among legal persons. It has three layers: responsibility, meaning clear role-based duties; answerability, meaning documentation, transparency, audit, and incident reporting; and enforceability, meaning redress, sanctions, and legal liability. I would therefore reject vague calls for the system itself to be accountable and instead require a RACI-style allocation with a named accountable executive and legal entity for each high-risk system. The EU AI Act (Regulation (EU) 2024/1689) illustrates this role-based model: providers have duties under Article 16, deployers under Article 26, fundamental-rights impact assessments under Article 27 for specified deployers, post-market monitoring under Article 72, and serious-incident reporting under Article 73. GDPR adds data-protection accountability through Articles 5, 22, 24, 28, and 82, covering lawful processing, automated decision-making, processor contracts, and compensation. The Council of Europe Framework Convention on AI (CETS 225) reinforces risk-based and rights-respecting answerability, while the OECD AI Principles, especially Principle 1.5 on accountability, and the UNESCO Recommendation on the Ethics of AI supply the policy baseline that actors are accountable according to their roles and context. Operationally, I would align company governance with the NIST AI Risk Management Framework, particularly its Govern function, and ISO/IEC 42001:2023 for an AI management system. Who or what should be accountable: providers and developers for safe design, data governance, conformity assessment, documentation, and monitoring; deployers and operators for context-specific risk assessment, human oversight, transparency to affected persons, and redress; executives and boards for resourcing, internal audit, and named ownership; and procurers and supply-chain actors for due diligence and contractual controls. AI models and systems are objects of documentation, evaluation, and control, not moral agents. For what: lawfulness and fundamental rights, safety and performance, transparency and explainability, data protection, non-discrimination, human oversight, security, incident response, and remedy. To whom: regulators and conformity bodies, courts, affected individuals and communities, workers and consumers, enterprise customers, shareholders, and the public. I would also use technical and procedural mechanisms such as documentation, model cards, bias audits, and reproducible evaluation, drawing on Kroll et al., Accountable Algorithms (2017), while recognizing Mittelstadt's warning that principles alone cannot guarantee ethical AI and Raji et al.'s argument for internal audit and closing the accountability gap. NYC Local Law 144 and State v. Loomis show how bias audits and due-process expectations around algorithmic evidence create concrete answerability, even where imperfect. My position as CEO is that accountability must be assigned before deployment, evidenced during operation, and enforceable after harm.",
