I1_Q3.text:
{
  "response": "As CEO, I would frame enforceability as the ability of a legal regime to turn an AI governance norm into a binding, observable, contestable, and remediable obligation. It has six elements: (1) legal basis and obligation — a statute, treaty, or contract creating a duty rather than a recommendation; (2) precision and operationalization — defined duty-bearers, triggers, thresholds, timelines, and acceptable evidence; (3) verification — audits, documentation, conformity assessment, incident reporting, and regulator access; (4) enforcement institutions — a regulator, court, procurement authority, or certification body with powers to inspect, order, fine, suspend, or grant relief; (5) remedies and due process — penalties, injunctions, damages, appeals, and rights to contest; and (6) de facto capacity — funding, technical expertise, and cross-border cooperation. A rule is merely aspirational when it uses hortatory language, lacks measurable duties or evidence, leaves compliance to self-assessment, and provides no standing, sanction, or remedy. Enforceability is a spectrum, not binary: hard law can be unenforceable if vague or under-resourced, while soft law can become enforceable through contracts, procurement conditions, certification, or incorporation by reference. The EU AI Act (Regulation (EU) 2024/1689) is enforceable in design because it creates prohibitions, high-risk provider obligations, conformity assessment and CE marking, market-surveillance authorities, and fines up to EUR 35 million or 7% of global turnover; its actual enforceability will depend on harmonized standards and national regulator capacity. GDPR Art. 22 and Art. 83 are enforceable through data-protection authorities and courts, and CJEU C-634/21 (SCHUFA) shows that automated credit scoring can be an Art. 22 decision, making transparency and human review justiciable. In the US, FTC Act Section 5 supports unfair or deceptive-practices enforcement, as in the FTC's Rite Aid facial-recognition order; Title VII supports disparate-impact liability; and NYC Local Law 144 and Colorado SB 24-205 create audit and impact-assessment duties. By contrast, the NIST AI Risk Management Framework is voluntary and aspirational unless a contract or procurement rule incorporates it. Loomis v. Wisconsin (Wis. 2016) illustrates procedural due-process limits on algorithmic risk scores but did not create a general AI rule. ECHR cases such as Glukhin v. Russia and the Dutch SyRI judgment show rights-based enforceability through courts requiring legal basis, necessity, and transparency. The Council of Europe Framework Convention on AI (CETS 225) is a treaty with obligations, but its practical force depends on domestic implementation and monitoring. OECD AI Principles and UNESCO's Recommendation are soft law. Theoretically, Abbott et al.'s legalization framework is useful: obligation, precision, and delegation determine whether a norm is hard or soft, and a rule with high obligation but low precision and low delegation is often aspirational in practice. Responsive regulation (Ayres and Braithwaite) and risk-based new-governance approaches explain how enforceable regimes combine ex ante controls, audit, and escalating sanctions. As CEO, I would support enforceable, interoperable, risk-based rules with clear safe harbors, third-party audits, incident reporting, and regulator capacity, because predictable enforcement is better for innovation than vague principles that expose companies to unpredictable liability.",
