I1_Q1.text:
{
  "response": "Legal certainty, as it applies to AI systems used in public decision-making, is the rule-of-law requirement that the legal basis for those systems, and the way they classify, score, prioritize, or recommend outcomes for individuals, be clear, accessible, stable, and foreseeable enough that people can anticipate how public power may affect them, rely on that understanding, and effectively challenge unlawful or arbitrary results. It does not require publication of every model parameter. It requires that automation not become a substitute for knowable law.\n\nIt requires several things. First, an accessible legal basis that states the system's purpose, the decisions it may inform, and the limits of its use. Under the European Court of Human Rights' quality-of-law doctrine in Sunday Times v. United Kingdom (1979), a norm counts as law only if it is formulated with sufficient precision for people to foresee, to a degree reasonable in the circumstances, the consequences of their conduct. The Venice Commission's Rule of Law Checklist (CDL-AD(2016)007) treats accessibility, foreseeability, stability, consistency, and protection of legitimate expectations as benchmarks of legal certainty. Second, the operative rules must themselves be clear and precise. The Court of Justice of the European Union, in Case 169/80 Gondrand Freres (1981), held that legal certainty requires rules of law to be clear and precise and their application foreseeable by those subject to them. Handing material criteria to an opaque or continually retuned model, without stable and published rules, fails that test. Third, people need proportionate information about the data categories used, the system's role in the decision, and the logic applied, plus reasons and a real route to review. The GDPR (Articles 5, 13-15, and 22) requires transparency and, for solely automated decisions with legal or similarly significant effects, safeguards that include meaningful information about the logic involved. The EU AI Act (Regulation (EU) 2024/1689) imposes record-keeping, transparency, and human-oversight duties on high-risk systems, including specified public-sector uses. French administrative law (Code des relations entre le public et l'administration, Article L. 311-3-1) gives individuals a right to the rules and principal characteristics of algorithmic processing that underlies an individual administrative decision. Article 41 of the EU Charter of Fundamental Rights requires good administration, including reasons. The Council of Europe Framework Convention on Artificial Intelligence and Human Rights, Democracy and the Rule of Law (CETS No. 225) ties public use of AI to the rule of law, transparency, accountability, and effective remedies. Fourth, material changes to models, thresholds, or criteria should not be applied retroactively to people who reasonably relied on the prior published framework. Danielle Keats Citron's Technological Due Process (2008) makes the related point that rules embedded in government code undermine notice and the opportunity to be heard when neither officials nor affected people can inspect them.\n\nLegal certainty is absent when a public body cannot explain, and a person cannot reasonably predict or reconstruct, why a benefit was denied, a risk flag raised, or a sanction proposed; when similarly situated people are treated inconsistently without a lawful justification; or when secrecy, proprietary or otherwise, blocks a challenge to accuracy, relevance, or bias. The District Court of The Hague's 2020 SyRI judgment (NJCM and others v. the State of the Netherlands) invalidated a welfare-fraud risk-profiling system in part because its operation was insufficiently transparent and foreseeable to satisfy Article 8 of the European Convention on Human Rights. State v. Loomis, 881 N.W.2d 749 (Wis. 2016), shows the same tension in sentencing: a proprietary risk tool may inform a decision only within due-process limits, and inability to examine how it weighs factors strains the person's capacity to contest the result. In that condition, automation does not merely implement the law; it hides the law.",
