I1_Q2.text:
{
  "response": "Accountability in AI governance is a relational and institutional duty, not a property of a model. Following Mark Bovens, it is a relationship in which an actor must explain and justify conduct to a forum that can question, judge, and impose consequences. Applied to AI, the relationship has to be specified across the lifecycle: who the actor is, for which decisions and harms, before which forum, and with what remedies. Technical explainability is only one means; documentation, traceability, oversight, and redress are equally necessary.\n\nWho should be accountable. Duties should attach to identifiable legal persons who exercise control, allocated by role rather than to “the algorithm.” Providers (those who develop a system or place it on the market) bear primary duties for design, training-data governance, testing, technical documentation, and instructions for use. Deployers (those who use a system under their authority) bear primary duties for whether the system is appropriate to the context, for human oversight, for how outputs are acted on, and for monitoring in operation. Importers, distributors, and operators of general-purpose or high-risk systems bear duties proportionate to their control and knowledge. Individuals inside organizations remain answerable through corporate governance, professional standards, and, in serious cases, personal liability, but a model cannot itself be a duty-bearer. States and regulators are accountable for the quality, independence, and effectiveness of oversight, not for every private deployment outcome.\n\nFor what. Accountability should cover: (1) design and development choices, including data governance, risk assessment, and evaluation; (2) deployment decisions, including fitness for context, human oversight, and instructions to users; (3) ongoing operation, logging, incident reporting, and correction or withdrawal when performance or impacts degrade; (4) compliance with applicable law, including data protection, non-discrimination, consumer and product-safety rules, and sectoral duties; and (5) harms to fundamental rights, safety, and legitimate interests, including where causation is probabilistic or mediated by a human decision-maker. Duties should scale with risk and with the degree of control, not with whether the system is labeled “AI.”\n\nTo whom. Affected persons—data subjects, consumers, workers, patients, and others whose rights or interests are engaged—should have rights to information, to an explanation adequate to the decision context, to human review of significant decisions, and to an effective remedy. Independent regulators and courts are the principal public forums, with powers to investigate, order correction, and impose sanctions. Where public bodies deploy AI, democratic institutions, auditors, and ombudsmen are additional forums. Because many harms are collective, individual standing should be supplemented by public-interest and collective mechanisms.\n\nThis allocation is already partly codified. The EU AI Act distinguishes providers and deployers and imposes risk management, data governance, technical documentation, logging, transparency, human oversight, and post-market monitoring, with market-surveillance authorities as a forum and penalties as consequences. The GDPR’s accountability principle in Article 5(2), the controller/processor structure, transparency and access rights in Articles 13–15, constraints on solely automated decisions in Article 22, and data-protection impact assessments in Article 35 allocate duties for personal-data processing and give data subjects and supervisory authorities standing. The OECD AI Principles and the UNESCO Recommendation on the Ethics of AI treat accountability as a lifecycle governance principle requiring traceability and human oversight. The NIST AI Risk Management Framework operationalizes related functions (govern, map, measure, manage) without itself creating liability. The Council of Europe Framework Convention on Artificial Intelligence requires parties to maintain accountability and redress for AI harms to human rights, democracy, and the rule of law. Case law such as State v. Loomis shows courts already functioning as forums when algorithmic tools affect liberty, while also showing the limits of disclosure as a substitute for answerability.\n\nScholarly work supplies the conceptual constraints. Bovens distinguishes accountability from mere transparency. Helen Nissenbaum identified how computerized systems fragment responsibility so that no one can be called to account. Wachter, Mittelstadt, and Floridi caution that a freestanding “right to explanation” is easily overstated and does not by itself produce remedy. Selbst and Barocas show that intuitive demands for explanation can miss the institutional question of who must justify what to whom. A sound framework therefore combines ex ante duties (risk and impact assessment, documentation, human oversight), ex post duties (logging, incident reporting, audit, liability, and remedy), and clear forums, allocated by control and role.",
