I1_Q3.text:
{
  "response": "Enforceability, applied to AI regulation, is the property that turns a norm into a duty whose breach can be detected, adjudicated, and remedied by an institution with legal authority. It is not the same as moral force, political consensus, or reputational pressure. A rule is enforceable when four conditions hold together: it is adopted in a form the legal system recognizes as binding; it imposes determinate obligations on identifiable actors; a competent authority has jurisdiction plus powers to investigate and decide; and non-compliance can end in a compulsory outcome—an order, penalty, injunction, market exclusion, or damages—that can actually be executed. An aspirational rule states goals or recommended practices without creating justiciable obligations or sanctions. Mandatory-sounding language does not by itself make a text enforceable.\n\nLegal theory supplies the distinction. H.L.A. Hart treated law as a union of primary rules of obligation and secondary rules of recognition, change, and adjudication. Many AI ethics codes have primary-looking principles but no secondary rules that tell officials what counts as law or how disputes are decided. Lon Fuller’s account of legality adds operational tests: generality, promulgation, clarity, consistency, possibility of compliance, stability, and congruence between official action and the declared rule. Open slogans such as “trustworthy” or “human-centric” AI fail clarity and congruence unless they are operationalized. Kenneth Abbott and Duncan Snidal’s hard-law/soft-law framework is especially useful for AI governance: instruments vary by obligation (binding versus hortatory), precision (determinate versus vague), and delegation (to a court, regulator, or monitoring body). Soft law can shape expectations and later legislation, but it is not enforceable on its own. Anu Bradford’s Brussels Effect explains a further practical dimension: market-access conditions can make formally territorial rules de facto binding on foreign firms that want to sell into the regulating market.\n\nBinding instruments show what enforceability looks like in AI-relevant law. The EU Artificial Intelligence Act (Regulation (EU) 2024/1689) is the clearest contemporary example of hard AI law: it prohibits specified practices, imposes duties on providers and deployers of high-risk systems and on general-purpose AI models, uses conformity assessment and market surveillance, and authorizes substantial administrative fines (including, for prohibited practices, up to €35 million or 7% of worldwide annual turnover). The GDPR is already an enforceable constraint on many AI systems that process personal data: independent supervisory authorities, investigative powers, administrative fines up to €20 million or 4% of global turnover, private judicial remedies, and Article 22 limits on solely automated decisions with legal or similarly significant effects. In the United States, enforceability is more fragmented. NIST’s AI Risk Management Framework is voluntary guidance; it becomes enforceable only if a statute, procurement rule, or contract incorporates it. State measures such as Colorado’s SB 24-205 and New York City Local Law 144 create more concrete duties, documentation or audit requirements, and public enforcement, though scope and remedies differ. Loomis v. Wisconsin (2016) shows courts engaging algorithmic risk scores under due process, and also the limit of judicial enforceability where trade secrecy and the absence of a statutory transparency duty constrain what a court will order.\n\nBy contrast, the OECD AI Principles and the UNESCO Recommendation on the Ethics of AI are soft-law benchmarks. They coordinate policy and supply a vocabulary for later law, but they do not themselves impose sanctions or private rights of action. The Council of Europe Framework Convention on Artificial Intelligence, Human Rights, Democracy and the Rule of Law is different in form: as a treaty it can create international legal obligations for parties, yet many duties are framed at a high level of generality, so practical enforceability depends on domestic implementation, monitoring, and whatever remedies national law supplies.\n\nThe operational contrast is therefore this. An enforceable AI rule names the obligor (provider, deployer, importer, public authority), states a standard that can be evidenced (logs, risk management, conformity assessment, bias audit, human oversight), designates a forum (regulator, court, or both), and attaches a remedy (fine, injunction, withdrawal, liability). An aspirational rule addresses “stakeholders,” uses unoperationalized values, relies on voluntary codes or certification, and treats compliance as a matter of reputation. A useful test: if an actor can ignore the text without facing a compulsory process that can end in a binding order or penalty, the text remains aspirational even if it uses the word “shall.” Conversely, a principle becomes enforceable when legislation, contract, or judicial doctrine supplies an obligor, a standard of conduct, a forum, and a remedy—and when the state has the capacity to monitor systems that are opaque, cross-border, and rapidly updated. Formal bindingness without monitoring capacity yields only weak enforceability; precision and institutional capacity are what separate a governable rule from a statement of intent.",
