{
  "response": "As the CEO of a major AI company in this working group, I define legal certainty for AI used in public decision-making as the practical ability of an affected person, the responsible official, and a reviewing court to know in advance which rules govern an AI-assisted decision, to foresee how those rules will be applied to like cases, and to obtain a reviewable account of why a particular outcome was reached. It is a rule-of-law requirement, not a demand that every model weight be published. It requires four things. First, a clear and accessible legal mandate: the authority to deploy the system, the purposes for which it may be used, and the interests it may affect must be set in law, not left to a procurement memo. Second, foreseeable and consistent application, so that similarly situated people can expect similar treatment and so that model, threshold, or data changes are versioned and documented rather than silent. Third, reason-giving sufficient for legality review—under the EU AI Act’s high-risk duties on transparency, logging, and human oversight; under GDPR Article 22’s limits on solely automated decisions that produce legal or similarly significant effects; and under constitutional due-process duties of the kind examined in State v. Loomis. Fourth, an effective remedy: notice, access to the material factors, and a human decision-maker who can depart from the system’s output and be held accountable for the decision. When legal certainty is absent, a benefits agency, court, or border authority relies on a proprietary score whose factors, thresholds, and error rates are undisclosed; officials can say only that “the system decided”; like cases diverge without a recorded reason; and the person affected cannot tell whether the result was lawful, biased, or the product of an unannounced software update. That is the breakdown Danielle Keats Citron called a failure of technological due process, and it is the concern that the Venice Commission’s Rule of Law Checklist treats as the core of legal certainty: law must be clear, stable, and predictable, and applied consistently. From an industry seat I will be plain: vendors must not sell opacity as a feature into the exercise of public power. Certainty is a design and contracting requirement—logged inputs, versioned models, contestable reasons, and terms that do not forbid the state from explaining its own decisions.",
