I1_Q3.text:
{
  "response": "From the standpoint of a company that will have to build, document, and defend systems under whatever framework this group produces, enforceability is not a rhetorical preference for toughness. It is the set of institutional features that turn a norm into a duty a regulated party can understand, a supervisor can test, and a court or authority can apply with predictable consequences. An enforceable AI rule changes conduct because noncompliance carries a real prospect of detection, correction, and sanction. An aspirational rule expresses a value—fairness, human-centricity, trustworthiness—without specifying who must do what, how compliance is shown, or what happens if it is not.\n\nFive features mark the difference. First, an identifiable duty-bearer: provider, deployer, importer, or public authority, not an undefined ecosystem. Second, an obligation specific enough to guide conduct and evidence—prohibited practices, risk-management and documentation duties, conformity assessment, logging, incident reporting, or transparency to affected persons—rather than an outcome no current method can measure, such as perfect fairness or zero error. Third, a competent authority with investigative and corrective powers, and a stated remedy: administrative fines, withdrawal or recall, injunctive relief, or civil liability. Fourth, procedure: standards of proof, rights of defense, and appeal, so enforcement itself is lawful. Fifth, possibility of compliance. A mandate that cannot be operationalized is aspiration wearing statutory clothing; it produces discretionary enforcement, not the rule of law.\n\nBinding instruments already show the pattern. The EU AI Act is a regulation with direct effect, a risk-based allocation of duties, market-surveillance authorities, and substantial administrative fines; whether one agrees with every obligation, the enforcement architecture is recognizable. The GDPR supplies the nearer precedent for cross-border digital regulation: independent supervisory authorities, turnover-based fines, and data-subject remedies, which is why firms treat it as law rather than guidance. In the United States, much AI-relevant enforcement still attaches to older, genuinely enforceable statutes—the FTC Act’s prohibition on unfair or deceptive practices has been used against misleading AI claims—rather than to a comprehensive AI code. State v. Loomis is a different but instructive constraint: due process limits how opaque algorithmic tools may be used when liberty is at stake. It enforces a constitutional boundary on government use; it does not itself create a private-sector compliance regime.\n\nSoft law sits on the other side of the line, and it is not useless. The OECD AI Principles and the UNESCO Recommendation on the Ethics of Artificial Intelligence shape expectations and often migrate into later statutes, but they create no justiciable duty and no penalty. NIST’s AI Risk Management Framework is technically serious and widely used; adherence may evidence diligence, yet non-adherence is not, by itself, a violation. Executive directives can bind agencies inside government while leaving private duties incomplete. Voluntary codes and principles-based charters belong in the aspirational category unless a legislature or regulator later incorporates them into a standard that can be audited and sanctioned.\n\nTheory draws the same line. H.L.A. Hart’s account of law distinguishes primary rules of obligation from secondary rules of recognition, change, and adjudication: a principle becomes enforceable only when institutions can identify it as law, apply it to cases, and back it with official pressure. Lon Fuller’s criteria for the inner morality of law—clarity, consistency, prospectivity, possibility of compliance, and congruence between the rule as announced and the rule as applied—are practical tests for this working group. A standard that fails those tests will be enforced unevenly or not at all, which is worse for both safety and innovation than an explicitly voluntary principle.\n\nFor governance design, the implication is selective bindingness. Use enforceable rules where harm is serious and duties can be specified and audited: bans on defined unacceptable practices, documented risk management for high-risk uses, human oversight where a statute assigns it, incident reporting, and truthful claims about capabilities. Keep principles, benchmarks, and maturity models explicitly non-binding where measurement is unstable, so that enforcement does not collapse into after-the-fact taste. Enforceability, in short, is specificity plus institutions plus consequences. Aspiration is a direction of travel. Both have a place; confusing them produces neither safety nor legal certainty.",
